WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.550
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: So
business is really heating up.

00:00:02.550 --> 00:00:06.000
Your users are downloading
the app, viewing your pages,

00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:07.530
clicking and tapping.

00:00:07.530 --> 00:00:09.060
Is it 10 times a second?

00:00:09.060 --> 00:00:09.840
1,000?

00:00:09.840 --> 00:00:11.520
Maybe it's a million.

00:00:11.520 --> 00:00:14.340
You're likely searching
for a messaging service

00:00:14.340 --> 00:00:17.010
to help you capture these
events and turn them

00:00:17.010 --> 00:00:19.650
into valuable
features and analysis.

00:00:19.650 --> 00:00:21.700
Stay tuned because
in today's episode,

00:00:21.700 --> 00:00:24.390
we are hoping you evaluate
the multiple messaging

00:00:24.390 --> 00:00:27.030
options available,
including Pub/Sub,

00:00:27.030 --> 00:00:28.560
to make your decision easy.

00:00:28.560 --> 00:00:34.910
[UPBEAT MUSIC]

00:00:34.910 --> 00:00:35.600
All right.

00:00:35.600 --> 00:00:37.790
So you are ready
to make your event

00:00:37.790 --> 00:00:40.400
data accessible
and useful, be it

00:00:40.400 --> 00:00:43.340
for automated machine
learning based recommendations

00:00:43.340 --> 00:00:45.560
or even just basic reporting.

00:00:45.560 --> 00:00:47.870
Building a data
pipeline is what will

00:00:47.870 --> 00:00:51.590
enable a smooth flow of
your source device data

00:00:51.590 --> 00:00:55.290
to your data warehouse or
downstream applications.

00:00:55.290 --> 00:00:58.640
The data pipeline automates
ingesting, transforming,

00:00:58.640 --> 00:01:02.000
storing, and routing
data to its destination.

00:01:02.000 --> 00:01:04.280
A pipeline looks
something like this,

00:01:04.280 --> 00:01:06.980
with the front end
landing zone for ingesting

00:01:06.980 --> 00:01:08.930
the data, a
continuous processing

00:01:08.930 --> 00:01:12.620
system such as Dataflow, Apache
Spark, or one custom built

00:01:12.620 --> 00:01:15.380
for transforming the
data, and finally, a data

00:01:15.380 --> 00:01:17.420
warehouse to store
and make the data

00:01:17.420 --> 00:01:20.450
available for analytics
or for routing data

00:01:20.450 --> 00:01:22.460
to a downstream application.

00:01:22.460 --> 00:01:26.150
In this video, we're focusing
on the ingestion layer of data

00:01:26.150 --> 00:01:28.520
pipeline and breaking
down your options

00:01:28.520 --> 00:01:31.010
so that you can make the
best ingestions service

00:01:31.010 --> 00:01:33.590
decision for your use case.

00:01:33.590 --> 00:01:35.630
When you collect
data from devices,

00:01:35.630 --> 00:01:39.380
each device will connect
to an API front-end.

00:01:39.380 --> 00:01:42.500
These front-ends might
buffer data into blobs

00:01:42.500 --> 00:01:46.310
before moving them to a blob
storage service such as Google

00:01:46.310 --> 00:01:49.520
Cloud Storage or Hadoop's HDFS.

00:01:49.520 --> 00:01:53.040
Buffering is critical to the
performance of these systems.

00:01:53.040 --> 00:01:55.220
But what if the
idea of buffering

00:01:55.220 --> 00:01:59.300
a large number of events on
your front-ends sounds risky

00:01:59.300 --> 00:02:02.750
or if you need that
data in real time?

00:02:02.750 --> 00:02:05.060
In this case, you need
a messaging service

00:02:05.060 --> 00:02:07.830
to land your events
as they happen.

00:02:07.830 --> 00:02:10.190
Google Cloud offers
messaging services

00:02:10.190 --> 00:02:13.700
that support streaming
data, Pub/Sub, Pub/Sub Lite,

00:02:13.700 --> 00:02:15.110
and Confluent Cloud.

00:02:15.110 --> 00:02:18.290
You can also run
Apache Kafka yourself.

00:02:18.290 --> 00:02:21.710
The easiest way to handle
messaging is Pub/Sub.

00:02:21.710 --> 00:02:23.750
As we've discussed
in this series,

00:02:23.750 --> 00:02:27.680
Pub/Sub is a service built
to make messaging just work,

00:02:27.680 --> 00:02:31.700
whether you send one message
a day or millions a second.

00:02:31.700 --> 00:02:35.510
With Pub/Sub, you create a
topic and publish messages to it

00:02:35.510 --> 00:02:37.610
from anywhere in the world.

00:02:37.610 --> 00:02:41.600
Anyone who needs to read these
messages creates a subscription

00:02:41.600 --> 00:02:44.180
and sees all messages
in one place,

00:02:44.180 --> 00:02:46.670
no matter where they originate.

00:02:46.670 --> 00:02:50.540
The readers and writers can
scale independently and have

00:02:50.540 --> 00:02:53.990
their own quotas, so
any team in your company

00:02:53.990 --> 00:02:57.510
that wants to do something with
that data can just have it.

00:02:57.510 --> 00:03:00.080
And for those writing
processing applications,

00:03:00.080 --> 00:03:02.510
Pub/Sub has one important trick.

00:03:02.510 --> 00:03:05.090
You can handle each
message independently,

00:03:05.090 --> 00:03:08.120
allowing more flexible
parallelism then

00:03:08.120 --> 00:03:10.790
you get with partition
based systems.

00:03:10.790 --> 00:03:13.040
Overall, this means
that you simply

00:03:13.040 --> 00:03:16.490
don't have to worry about how
your messages are ingested

00:03:16.490 --> 00:03:19.820
once you teach your front-end
to publish to Pub/Sub.

00:03:19.820 --> 00:03:22.250
Of course, it's not
just about ingestion.

00:03:22.250 --> 00:03:24.590
For large scale
stateful processing

00:03:24.590 --> 00:03:28.250
such as pipelines that
aggregate data intercessions

00:03:28.250 --> 00:03:31.790
or join multiple streams, as
well as scalable connectors

00:03:31.790 --> 00:03:35.000
to databases such as
BigQuery and BigTable,

00:03:35.000 --> 00:03:37.580
Pub/Sub is integrated
with Dataflow.

00:03:37.580 --> 00:03:40.880
Dataflow is its own
story, but suffice

00:03:40.880 --> 00:03:44.930
it to say that you can create
an auto scaled pipeline,

00:03:44.930 --> 00:03:48.950
pushing data to storage
with a few clicks in the UI

00:03:48.950 --> 00:03:51.170
or write complex
processing logic

00:03:51.170 --> 00:03:54.810
in Java, Python, SQL,
and other languages

00:03:54.810 --> 00:03:57.890
and have Dataflow run the
code reliably for you.

00:03:57.890 --> 00:04:00.450
There's another cool
thing that Pub/Sub can do,

00:04:00.450 --> 00:04:04.770
which is supporting message
delivery to webhooks natively.

00:04:04.770 --> 00:04:07.490
So you can do lightweight
but highly scalable

00:04:07.490 --> 00:04:11.900
data processing and forwarding
in Cloud Functions, App Engine

00:04:11.900 --> 00:04:14.960
or a custom app you run in GKE.

00:04:14.960 --> 00:04:18.860
Pub/Sub does a lot, so it can
be expensive for some use cases.

00:04:18.860 --> 00:04:21.529
As an alternative,
Pub/Sub Lite provides

00:04:21.529 --> 00:04:25.190
a cost effective service for
real time data streaming.

00:04:25.190 --> 00:04:28.310
Working with Pub/Sub Lite
has some notable differences

00:04:28.310 --> 00:04:29.750
from working with Pub/Sub.

00:04:29.750 --> 00:04:33.980
First, Lite has single zone data
storage, rather than multi zone

00:04:33.980 --> 00:04:35.960
as in the case of Pub/Sub.

00:04:35.960 --> 00:04:39.470
Second, Lite relies on
partitions or shards

00:04:39.470 --> 00:04:42.690
to achieve parallelism
and horizontal scaling.

00:04:42.690 --> 00:04:45.020
This is less flexible
than Pub/Sub's model

00:04:45.020 --> 00:04:47.810
of [? pure ?] message
lifecycle tracking.

00:04:47.810 --> 00:04:51.230
And third, with Lite,
you pay for the capacity

00:04:51.230 --> 00:04:55.440
reserved for your partitions,
whether you use it or not.

00:04:55.440 --> 00:04:58.010
So there is some
operational overhead

00:04:58.010 --> 00:05:00.200
to optimize capacity planning.

00:05:00.200 --> 00:05:04.380
A bonus of Lite is that it will
be familiar to Kafka users.

00:05:04.380 --> 00:05:06.560
In fact, there's
even a client library

00:05:06.560 --> 00:05:09.470
that implements the
Kafka client API.

00:05:09.470 --> 00:05:13.250
This similarity also allows
us to provide an integration

00:05:13.250 --> 00:05:16.880
with Apache Spark's structured
streaming, which is commonly

00:05:16.880 --> 00:05:18.500
used with Kafka.

00:05:18.500 --> 00:05:22.580
Like Pub/Sub, Lite is integrated
with data flow for processing.

00:05:22.580 --> 00:05:26.660
It does not, however, offer
push delivery to webhooks.

00:05:26.660 --> 00:05:29.930
Speaking of Apache
Kafka, no discussion

00:05:29.930 --> 00:05:33.380
of real time streaming
is complete without it.

00:05:33.380 --> 00:05:36.950
So your options include
running Kafka yourself

00:05:36.950 --> 00:05:40.160
or using Confluent
Cloud, a managed service

00:05:40.160 --> 00:05:42.110
from the founders of Kafka.

00:05:42.110 --> 00:05:45.950
Running Kafka yourself is
flexible and can be very cheap.

00:05:45.950 --> 00:05:49.580
In addition, Kafka comes with
a rich ecosystem of tools

00:05:49.580 --> 00:05:51.680
that are simple to
deploy, connectors

00:05:51.680 --> 00:05:55.400
to databases such as
BigQuery-- but operating Kafka

00:05:55.400 --> 00:05:59.240
requires a substantial
commitment to administration.

00:05:59.240 --> 00:06:01.430
Confluent provides
all the benefits

00:06:01.430 --> 00:06:06.070
of Kafka ecosystem with
managed services and support.

00:06:06.070 --> 00:06:08.360
When you choose between
Kafka and Pub/Sub,

00:06:08.360 --> 00:06:10.910
it is worth considering
these factors.

00:06:10.910 --> 00:06:13.250
Familiarity and
existing software.

00:06:13.250 --> 00:06:15.890
If you already have
experience running Kafka

00:06:15.890 --> 00:06:18.470
as well as applications
that rely on it,

00:06:18.470 --> 00:06:20.990
it may make sense to
just stick with it.

00:06:20.990 --> 00:06:23.180
Availability outside
of Google Cloud--

00:06:23.180 --> 00:06:26.420
if you're planning to
run a multi-Cloud system,

00:06:26.420 --> 00:06:28.520
Kafka may be a better
choice since it

00:06:28.520 --> 00:06:31.910
is available on
virtually all platforms.

00:06:31.910 --> 00:06:34.340
Integration with Google
Cloud- now Pub/Sub

00:06:34.340 --> 00:06:36.530
is a first party
service, which means

00:06:36.530 --> 00:06:40.130
a consistent authentication
administration security

00:06:40.130 --> 00:06:43.550
features as well as
consistent support experience.

00:06:43.550 --> 00:06:47.090
Access control-- when you have
multiple teams or companies

00:06:47.090 --> 00:06:50.510
that need to work with the same
data, Pub/Sub's resource that

00:06:50.510 --> 00:06:53.420
will access control and
administration system

00:06:53.420 --> 00:06:55.170
can be very helpful.

00:06:55.170 --> 00:06:57.560
In addition to a
flexible sharing model,

00:06:57.560 --> 00:07:00.920
you also get independent
[? quota ?] and billing, even

00:07:00.920 --> 00:07:04.080
between readers and
writers to the same topic.

00:07:04.080 --> 00:07:07.640
In addition, you don't have to
think about different clusters

00:07:07.640 --> 00:07:08.840
of popping up.

00:07:08.840 --> 00:07:13.190
There is only one global
Pub/Sub of service.

00:07:13.190 --> 00:07:16.580
Unpredictable growth-- now one
of the advantages of Pub/Sub

00:07:16.580 --> 00:07:20.210
is that it does not make you
commit to a fixed capacity,

00:07:20.210 --> 00:07:23.210
be it cluster size or
number of partitions.

00:07:23.210 --> 00:07:26.540
This won't matter much if
your workload is steady,

00:07:26.540 --> 00:07:30.170
but if you are growing quickly
or see spikes in the workload,

00:07:30.170 --> 00:07:32.730
this model is very convenient.

00:07:32.730 --> 00:07:34.970
So what's the TLDR?

00:07:34.970 --> 00:07:38.240
When it comes to picking a
service to ingest and land

00:07:38.240 --> 00:07:40.790
data, use Google
Cloud Storage when

00:07:40.790 --> 00:07:45.500
extremely low cost is high
priority and freshness is not.

00:07:45.500 --> 00:07:48.140
The downside is that
you keep more data

00:07:48.140 --> 00:07:52.250
on the front-ends, risking data
loss and complicated scaling.

00:07:52.250 --> 00:07:55.190
For real time streaming
options within Google Cloud,

00:07:55.190 --> 00:07:57.320
you have Pub/Sub
and Pub/Sub Lite.

00:07:57.320 --> 00:08:01.070
Pub/Sub is truly everything
that you need for most real time

00:08:01.070 --> 00:08:04.220
cases, integrating seamlessly
with other Google Cloud

00:08:04.220 --> 00:08:05.300
Services.

00:08:05.300 --> 00:08:08.390
Pub/Sub aims for a
no ops experience

00:08:08.390 --> 00:08:12.920
with automatic scaling and pay
for usage rather than capacity.

00:08:12.920 --> 00:08:15.770
However, because
Pub/Sub does a lot,

00:08:15.770 --> 00:08:18.170
it's not the cheapest
messaging solution.

00:08:18.170 --> 00:08:20.030
When it comes to
cost efficiency,

00:08:20.030 --> 00:08:24.110
Pub/Sub Lite delivers
scalable, real-time messaging

00:08:24.110 --> 00:08:25.730
at low cost.

00:08:25.730 --> 00:08:28.970
Like Pub/Sub, it has good
support with Apache Beam

00:08:28.970 --> 00:08:31.280
and Spark for
processing and will be

00:08:31.280 --> 00:08:33.559
more familiar to Kafka users.

00:08:33.559 --> 00:08:36.650
It is, however, not
entirely no ops.

00:08:36.650 --> 00:08:39.470
You will have to manage
your own capacity.

00:08:39.470 --> 00:08:42.799
And there you have it, the
top tools and characteristics

00:08:42.799 --> 00:08:46.310
to consider when building
out your data pipeline.

00:08:46.310 --> 00:08:49.040
Hopefully we've made it easier
to pick a messaging service

00:08:49.040 --> 00:08:51.080
for your event driven workload.

00:08:51.080 --> 00:08:53.690
Which one of these messaging
options are you using?

00:08:53.690 --> 00:08:55.430
Share with me in
the comments below

00:08:55.430 --> 00:08:58.760
and ask us questions on
the Pub/Sub discuss list.

00:08:58.760 --> 00:09:01.610
Thank you for joining us
today on Pub/Sub Made Easy.

00:09:01.610 --> 00:09:06.760
[UPBEAT MUSIC]

